349:**Matched control input.** The matched tape has four fields only: the delivered stimulus, the external teaching input, the event kind (lesson or replay) and the order. Every row carries a pulse: the lesson rule already excludes trials without one, and each tape comes from the diary fly it is paired with, whose replays always carry that fly's own pulse, so no pulse-free row can appear. Replay logs (dopamine factors, update sizes, the chosen entry) are fly-internal and are not on the tape. Each control arm with a diary receives, for the fly it is compared with, the tape up to the exact receipt cutoff of that fly's selected snapshot, including whether that snapshot's last replay was applied; no event after the cutoff is given. Each control then uses the tape as follows: the final-diary lookup reads that fly's frozen diary at the cutoff; the full-history lookup has one entry per original lesson receipt (replays are never stored as cases); the simple learners apply every tape event as an update. The protocol's missing-idea and filler conditions are kept as the protocol defines them, so a control that lacks an idea never receives the omitted lessons. Fly arms choose for themselves, so they see different lessons; for the central attribution test, the learner arms are matched to the tape of the evaluated full diary fly. The auditor checks byte equality of the matched tape for each comparison, separately from each arm's own update records, and checks the diary bytes each lookup consumes against the selected checkpoint's manifest hash (section 4.7), so a later diary cannot pass.
